  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 134 PM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nThe forecast begins with a cold front located along a line from \nHebron, Nebraska to Red Oak, Iowa. Colder air is pushing into the \nregion behind the front with temperatures expected to continue to \nfall through the rest of the afternoon. Light rain continues to \nincrease in coverage across eastern Nebraska/western Iowa \nthrough the remainder of the afternoon and into this evening. \nExpect rain to taper off northwest to southeast by early Sunday \nmorning. There is a small chance of hearing a few rumbles of \nthunder across extreme southeastern Nebraska this afternoon, \nwhere there is limited instability.\n\nLow pressure over the Dakotas and a shortwave trough over \nKansas/Oklahoma both move east through the course of the day Sunday, \nwith zonal flow setting up over the region. Expect cooler \ntemperatures, with highs topping out in the low to mid-40s for most \nlocations. A few areas in northeastern Nebraska may struggle to \nreach the upper 30s. \n\nThe cooler temperatures stick around through mid-week as high \npressure drops in from the north. Dry conditions are expected along \nwith unseasonably cool temperatures. Highs will be primarily in the \n30s and 40s each day while morning lows will bottom out in the teens \nand 20s. \n\nLooking ahead to Thanksgiving day, expect a dry and slightly warmer \nday. Highs are currently forecast to reach the low to mid-40s.",
